SF-304 — the citation check exists and nothing inherits it
==========================================================

Target:   skills/spec-double-2/scripts/check_citations.py (shipping it),
          skills/spec-double-2/scripts/scaffold_spec_workflow.py (the convention)
Unit:     tla-spec-dev
Proposed by: SI-05 (issue #338).

STATUS SPLIT — read this before treating the finding as open or closed:

  * The CONVENTION half is APPLIED by SI-05. scaffold_spec_workflow.py now
    writes the "cite symbols, not line numbers" convention into the scaffolded
    `desired/README.md` and `current/README.md`. That file is inside SI-05's
    conflict keys, so the change is in this PR.
  * The EXECUTABLE half is proposed below and NOT applied. Wiring a check into
    the scaffold means deciding what a scaffolded project runs it FROM, which is
    a scaffold-contract decision SI-05 does not own.

Measurement behind this, taken on the epic tip (994f650c): a grep of the entire
skill surface finds exactly one mention of `check_citations` -- the script
itself. No reference page, no scaffold, no template, no test outside this
repository's own suite refers to it. So the RC-02 fix is repository-local, while
the finding's own diagnosis is that this "is a skill-level pattern, not a
repository one".

Corroboration that the pattern is still live HERE: SI-05's own pre-change
baseline (pytest-before.txt) carries three failing cases of
`tests/test_source_citations.py::test_every_line_citation_resolves_to_the_line_it_cites`
-- on specs/current, specs/program_model and specs/desired_program_model
spec_manifest.yaml. The check works; the drift it was built for is ongoing.

PROPOSED, the executable half
-----------------------------

Give a scaffolded project the check and a way to run it:

--- a/skills/spec-double-2/scripts/scaffold_spec_workflow.py
+++ b/skills/spec-double-2/scripts/scaffold_spec_workflow.py
@@ scaffold
     write_if_missing(results / ".gitkeep", "")
+
+    # SF-304: ship the check, not only the convention. A scaffolded project
+    # gets a runnable citation check over its own spec tree, so comment drift
+    # goes red in the project that has the comments rather than only here.
+    write_if_missing(
+        specs_dir / "check_citations.md",
+        "# Citation check\n\n"
+        "Run `tla-spec-dev check citations --spec-root <specs>` to verify that\n"
+        "every line citation in this tree still resolves to the line it cites.\n"
+        "Advisory: it reports and exits 0.\n",
+    )
     return specs_dir

and register the subcommand that page names, wrapping the existing
`scripts/check_citations.py` surface:

    tla-spec-dev check citations [--spec-root specs] [--scope <paths>]

DELIBERATELY ADVISORY. `check_citations.py` currently backs a repository test
that FAILS on drift. A scaffolded project should get the report and exit 0 --
the epic's GOAL-no-new-gates forbids adding a refusal, and a brand-new project
inheriting a hard failure on its first scaffold is exactly the "a gate here
reads to an agent as a stop" shape the epic is removing.

THE CHEAPER ALTERNATIVE, if the scaffold contract should not grow: drop the
executable half entirely and keep only the convention SI-05 applied. The finding
explicitly offers that as an either/or ("ship the check (or its convention)"),
and the convention alone already prevents the drift in new projects. What must
not survive is the current state, where the skill knows the pattern, owns a
working checker, and hands neither to the projects it scaffolds.
